From fac674262e5a14d061f9973fc6c8861af28fe1b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Vin=C3=ADcius?= <viniciusmoraes.r@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 12:25:49 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] ac/gpu_info: make PCIe 4.0 and 5.0 lane bandwidth exact
 multiples of 3.0

radv places command buffers in VRAM only if pcie_bandwidth_mbps is at
least 8 * 0.985 * 1024 = 8069 ("PCIe 3.0 x8"). A PCIe 4.0 x4 link has
the same bandwidth but computed to 4 * 1.969 * 1024 = 8065, because
1.969 is not exactly twice 0.985 (0.98461 rounded up, 1.96923 rounded
down). So cards in a chipset-attached x4 slot got GTT command buffers.
PCIe 5.0 x2 failed the same way.

PCIe 4.0 and 5.0 use the same 128b/130b encoding as 3.0, so use
exactly 2x and 4x its per-lane value. PCIe 3.0 x8, 4.0 x4 and 5.0 x2
now all compute to 8069; no other generation/lane combination changes
side.

Measured on an RX 7900 XTX in a chipset PCIe 4.0 x4 slot: llama.cpp
Vulkan decode goes from 204.9 to 221.6 t/s (+8.2%) on gemma-4-E2B
and 75.4 to 79.0 (+4.9%) on gemma-4-12B, matching RADV_PERFTEST=sam
on the unpatched build.

Fixes: 95d06343c693 ("radv: Don't place CS in VRAM when bandwidth is too low.")
Closes: https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa/-/work_items/16298
---
 src/amd/common/ac_gpu_info.c | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/amd/common/ac_gpu_info.c b/src/amd/common/ac_gpu_info.c
index b11a7791a43d..209253c8b071 100644
--- a/src/amd/common/ac_gpu_info.c
+++ b/src/amd/common/ac_gpu_info.c
@@ -1669,10 +1669,10 @@ ac_query_gpu_info(int fd, void *dev_p, struct radeon_info *info,
       info->pcie_bandwidth_mbps = info->pcie_num_lanes * 0.985 * 1024;
       break;
    case 4:
-      info->pcie_bandwidth_mbps = info->pcie_num_lanes * 1.969 * 1024;
+      info->pcie_bandwidth_mbps = info->pcie_num_lanes * 1.970 * 1024;
       break;
    case 5:
-      info->pcie_bandwidth_mbps = info->pcie_num_lanes * 3.938 * 1024;
+      info->pcie_bandwidth_mbps = info->pcie_num_lanes * 3.940 * 1024;
       break;
    case 6:
       info->pcie_bandwidth_mbps = info->pcie_num_lanes * 7.563 * 1024;
